# What the guest image is built from, exactly.
#
# Every developer's sandbox is meant to be the same sandbox. That is only true
# across releases if the image is built from the same bytes every time, and
# naming a version is not enough to get that: Alpine's branch directory holds
# whatever the branch currently is, so a point release replaces the tarballs
# and a package bump replaces the .apk, in place, under the same names.
#
# So the release is pinned, the branch path is derived from it rather than
# following latest-stable, and everything downloaded is checked against the
# sha256 recorded here. A mirror that serves something else stops the build
# instead of producing an image nobody can account for.
#
# The packages (kind apk) are also kept, byte for byte, in apk/<arch> beside
# this file, because the branch repository drops a package's pinned build as
# soon as a newer one is published. The release tarballs (kind file) are not
# kept: Alpine keeps releases/ indefinitely.
#
# Moving to a new Alpine is a deliberate act, and it is one command:
#
#     make guest-pins ALPINE=3.25.0
#
# which re-resolves the package closure, downloads everything for both
# architectures and rewrites the lines below with what it actually got. Read
# the diff — it is the changelog for the machine every tool call runs in.
#
# Lines are: <arch> <kind> <name> <sha256>.
alpine 3.24.1
